{"title":"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Additional_observations\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Additional observations</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/4/054090e60a5dcb8bc33213620b3af9278f9a3e12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2010$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" /> positive numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/b/a3b3f9b76b59f3b05d0e1a95bbc608d107831e3a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, \\ldots , a_{2010}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"121\" height=\"13\" /> satisfy\nthe inequality <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/3/8330a611191a159662da53a14df9564a37b6eacb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_ia_j \\le i+j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"92\" height=\"17\" /> for all distinct indices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/1/3b1be2340079411dac99664d3857725babacc96e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i, j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"22\" height=\"16\" />.\nDetermine, with proof, the largest possible value of the product\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/b/78b9d0e572dc18a873644028eb76b18bf2a05342.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1a_2\\cdots a_{2010}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"96\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The largest possible value is\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/1/1c1f8befc2d0f10133da7bd6e89341ecd44eb99a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\prod_{i=1}^{1005}(4i-1) = 3\\times 7 \\times \\ldots \\times 4019.\\]\" width=\"256\" height=\"50\" />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>No larger value is possible, since for each consecutive pair of elements:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/5/3351cbada60a9d26e0d18d3938c0b2ae67e30570.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a_{2i-1},a_{2i}), 1\\le i \\le 1005$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"190\" height=\"18\" />, the product is at most\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/d/3/7d3b2fa01f292a639b4df2b51fabdc77eba20090.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2i-1) + 2i = 4i - 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"168\" height=\"18\" />, and so the product of all the pairs is at most:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/f/56f960bad040442806b2997326ff3c2a56182b03.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\prod_{i=1}^{1005}(4i-1).\\]\" width=\"89\" height=\"50\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>If we can demonstrate a sequence in which for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/f/a8f7724ada8970a2956818b61ea9be8c81ac7f0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\le i \\le 1005$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"102\" height=\"16\" /> the product\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/b/e8bbe358bc4af9f8717680017b4661c7be502a03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{2i-1}a_{2i} = 4i-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"130\" height=\"15\" />, and all the inequalities are satisfied, the above\nupper bound will be achieved and the proof complete.\n</p><p>We will construct sequences of an arbitrarily large even length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/9/fe9092c5576b16e500819564fa41940124075eb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n \\ge 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" />,\nin which:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/4/8a4c28a9cc5e8e0f102afb0e5a5934edcb214af0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{cases}   a_ia_j = i+j = 2i+1 &amp; j=i+1, \\\\   a_ia_j = i+j = 2n-2 &amp; j = i+2 = 2n, \\\\   a_ia_j &lt; i+j            &amp; i \\ne 2n-2, \\mbox{ and } i &lt; j-1. \\end{cases}\\]\" width=\"399\" height=\"74\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab39d761413804680d26d972381f028001562f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" />, from the equations <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/5/cd5fae15b6e1a1f4a62b1fba7d6aed8f1822ff8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_ia_{i+1} = 2i+1,\\; 1\\le i\\le 2n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"245\" height=\"16\" />,\nwe obtain the whole sequence recursively:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/4/a84f7c7fe38abe14dfef2f7077cd651543961ad5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1 = a_1,\\; a_2 = 3/a_1,\\; a_3 = 5/a_2 = 5a_1/3,\\; a_4 = 7/a_3 = (3\\cdot 7)/(5a_1) \\ldots.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"549\" height=\"20\" /> And as a result:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/1/0f13a270d6e0ba69d88f2168121d8c2e9afbd46d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_{i+2} = a_i \\cdot \\frac{2i+3}{2i+1}\\quad 1 \\le i \\le 2n-2.\\]\" width=\"270\" height=\"39\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>The same equations <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/f/58f7441d1a9970233aca9d0a033e911b618db712.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_ia_{i+1} = 2i+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"117\" height=\"16\" /> can be used to compute the\nwhole sequence from any other known term.\n</p><p>We will often need to compare fractions in which the numerator and denominator\nare both positive, with fractions in which a positive term is added to both.\nSuppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/6/48689d638ddca59030006d362cbd848607f63aa2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p, q, r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"11\" /> are three positive real numbers, then:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/b/f3b63fa25913edf863409835b992226c7df73700.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} p &amp;&gt; q \\implies &amp; \\frac{p+r}{q+r} &amp;&lt; \\frac{p}{q} \\\\ p &amp;&lt; q \\implies &amp; \\frac{p+r}{q+r} &amp;&gt; \\frac{p}{q} \\\\    &amp;  \\mbox{and always} &amp; \\frac{p+r}{q} &amp;&gt; \\frac{p}{q} \\end{align*}\" width=\"273\" height=\"130\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Returning to the problem in hand, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/8/478f5d7d31a103e7a383486cdf317eb599ac9ebf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i &lt; j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"38\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/5/1e51c727c9daabdf8901a5faa0005e9ab6eacb97.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_ia_j \\le i+j \\implies a_ia_{j+2} &lt; i+j+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"283\" height=\"17\" />.\nIf it were otherwise, we would have for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/8/478f5d7d31a103e7a383486cdf317eb599ac9ebf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i &lt; j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"38\" height=\"16\" />:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/b/00b430d3409353dcb459059f94ae837f19870130.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{a_{j+2}}{a_j} \\ge \\frac{i+j+2}{i+j} &gt; \\frac{j+j+2}{j+j} = \\frac{2j+2}{2j} &gt; \\frac{2j+3}{2j+1} = \\frac{a_{j+2}}{a_j},\\]\" width=\"451\" height=\"42\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>so our assumption is impossible.\n</p><p>Therefore, we need only verify inequalities with an index difference of\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, as these imply the rest.\n</p><p>Now, when the indices differ by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> we have ensured equality (and\nhence the desired inequalities) by construction. So, we only need\nto prove the inequalities for successive even index and successive\nodd index pairs, i.e. for every index <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/0/aa001d833faade905df65b73d615b0e97bd3f555.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i &gt; 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" />, prove <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/d/d4d33979df4513ed448c84f54c4f86bc4edb151f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{i-2}a_i \\le 2i-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"117\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>We now compare <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/b/3fb7a54fd8f0e0684737f294a70f6e5145d13990.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_ia_{i+2}/(2i+2)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"115\" height=\"18\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/e/82ece733f4d085da903c3544382347791c9479b3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{i+2}a_{i+4}/(2i+6)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"132\" height=\"18\" />. By our\nrecurrence relations:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/6/826a0aa9b8ab8c5e820a7740f77780cf7c26aeca.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}   \\frac{a_{i+2}a_{i+4}}{2i+6}     &amp;= \\frac{a_ia_{i+2}}{2i+2} \\cdot          \\frac{2i+2}{2i+6} \\cdot          \\frac{a_{i+4}}{a_{i+2}}\\cdot          \\frac{a_{i+2}}{a_i} \\\\     &amp;= \\frac{a_ia_{i+2}}{2i+2}\\cdot          \\frac{i+1}{i+3}\\cdot          \\frac{2i+7}{2i+5}\\cdot          \\frac{2i+3}{2i+1} \\\\     &amp;= \\frac{a_ia_{i+2}}{2i+2}\\cdot          \\frac{(i+1)(2i+7)(2i+3)}{(i+3)(2i+5)(2i+1)} \\\\     &amp;= \\frac{a_ia_{i+2}}{2i+2}\\cdot          \\frac{4i^3+24i^2+41i+21}{4i^3+24i^2+41i+15} \\\\     &amp;= \\frac{a_ia_{i+2}}{2i+2}\\cdot          \\left(1+\\frac{6}{4i^3+24i^2+41i+15}\\right) \\\\     &amp;&gt; \\frac{a_ia_{i+2}}{2i+2}. \\end{align*}\" width=\"376\" height=\"273\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>So, for both odd and even index pairs, the strict inequality\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/a/f5a9eb4aec46a86150fef1bc92045e56c7a7b529.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_ia_{i+2} &lt; 2i+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"117\" height=\"16\" /> follows from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/1/6415bd62c97464ce319ca1c451dbb981e2bedf66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{i+2}a_{i+4} \\le 2i+6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"134\" height=\"16\" />\nand we need only prove the inequalities <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/c/12c03487e26ee8c6c05919d34d9d07b764010524.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{2n-3}a_{2n-1} \\le 4n-4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"160\" height=\"14\" /> and\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/0/a707d669105dd4d0ab679809a264913e357d35aa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{2n-2}a_{2n} \\le 4n-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"145\" height=\"18\" />, the second of which holds (as an equality)\nby construction, so only the first remains.\n</p><p>We have not yet used the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/8/e68e7fdfb031159ec0a98ab146bcc8ca9db9fe77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{2n-2}a_{2n} = 4n-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"142\" height=\"14\" />, with this\nwe can solve for the last three terms (or equivalently their squares)\nand thus compute the whole sequence. From the equations:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/f/32f2c4235732928b00fd2fe3475ab734897393fd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}   a_{2n-1}a_{2n} &amp;= 4n-1 \\\\   a_{2n-2}a_{2n} &amp;= 4n-2 \\\\   a_{2n-2}a_{2n-1} &amp;= 4n-3 \\end{align*}\" width=\"159\" height=\"64\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>multiplying any two and dividing by the third, we get:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/d/fcdbe6c99afcf3b27f99edc38c127151e8d6b964.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}   a_{2n}^2     &amp;= \\frac{(4n-2)(4n-1)}{4n-3} \\\\   a_{2n-1}^2  &amp;= \\frac{(4n-3)(4n-1)}{4n-2} \\\\   a_{2n-2}^2  &amp;= \\frac{(4n-3)(4n-2)}{4n-1} \\end{align*}\" width=\"199\" height=\"132\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>from which,\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/6/e169ffea0e864b5ead57172b8015928f7ae96867.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a_{2n-3}^2 = \\frac{(4n-5)^2}{a_{2n-2}^2} = \\frac{(4n-5)^2(4n-1)}{(4n-3)(4n-2)}\\]\" width=\"307\" height=\"43\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>With the squares of the last four terms in hand, we can now verify\nthe only non-redundant inequality:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/b/28b22337235ff0caacf849aab5b960e6f3572db4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} a_{2n-3}^2a_{2n-1}^2   &amp;= \\frac{(4n-5)^2}{a_{2n-2}^2}a_{2n-1}^2 \\\\   &amp;= \\left(\\frac{(4n-5)(4n-1)}{4n-2}\\right)^2 \\\\   &amp;= \\left(\\frac{16n^2 -24n + 5}{4n-2}\\right)^2 \\\\   &amp;&lt; \\left(\\frac{16n^2 -24n + 8}{4n-2}\\right)^2 \\\\   &amp;= (4n-4)^2. \\end{align*}\" width=\"275\" height=\"229\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>The inequality above follows because the numerator and denominator are both positive for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/d/49d98ffe996c0f0d3b82b284cba2113439fefea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>This completes the construction and the proof of all the inequalities,\nwhich miraculously reduced to just one inequality for the last pair\nof odd indices.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Additional_observations\">Additional observations</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Additional observations\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>If we choose a different first term, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/3/5930e3addb4c17fb28c6ff379a5921469d778c8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1&#39; = M\\cdot a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"89\" height=\"18\" />, the\nsequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/b/31be5a8d83a30d5f18ff37ec80470e645e753716.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"14\" height=\"18\" /> will have the form:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/9/3a92ec157d1a59b5e8bb9c16fa2496d1bc449104.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \ta_{2i-1}&#39; &amp;= Ma_{2i-1} \\\\ \ta_{2i}&#39; &amp;= \\frac{a_{2i}}{M} \\end{align*}\" width=\"118\" height=\"59\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>the same holds if we have a longer sequence, at every index of the\nshorter sequence, the longer sequence will be a constant multiple\n(for all the odd terms) or dividend (for all the even terms)\nof the corresponding term of shorter sequence.\n</p><p>We observe that our solution is not unique, indeed for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/e/67e6630318d8cb28de2723180b6d359b972489c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"13\" />,\nthe same construction with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/e/22ee54dd4a34719980d5f378e295d962b40d2467.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n+2k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> terms, truncated to just the\nfirst <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/3/f93f5c51ea4b04b4992b003b39479f29018f6bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> terms, yields a sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/a/e4aed80b8a4d8604d976f59fb5668419bba3b8ff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&#39;_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"14\" height=\"18\" /> which also satisfies all\nthe required conditions, but in this case <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/7/9c703d7e6ba18ca8fbd351be240eae2317edb814.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a&#39;_{2n-2}a&#39;{2n} &lt; 4n-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"151\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>We could have constructed this alternative solution directly,\nby replacing the right hand side in the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/8/e68e7fdfb031159ec0a98ab146bcc8ca9db9fe77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{2n-2}a_{2n} = 4n-2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"142\" height=\"14\" />\nwith any smaller value for which we still get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/c/12c03487e26ee8c6c05919d34d9d07b764010524.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{2n-3}a_{2n-1} \\le 4n-4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"160\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>In the modified construction, for some constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/c/98c67dfea510f6dd7917da627612652702339b24.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"52\" height=\"13\" />, we have:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/a/6ba7118b0cfa0180118550089b836822042f5402.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \ta_{2n}^2 &amp;= \\frac{1}{M}\\cdot \\frac{(4n-2)(4n-1)}{4n-3} \\\\ \ta_{2n-1}^2 &amp;= M\\cdot \\frac{(4n-3)(4n-1)}{4n-2} \\\\ \ta_{2n-2}^2 &amp;= \\frac{1}{M}\\cdot \\frac{(4n-3)(4n-2)}{4n-1} \\end{align*}\" width=\"235\" height=\"132\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>and so:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/2/47281ffd27c127610e14cd7d182cca10fe27053b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \ta_{2n-3}^2a_{2n-1}^2 &amp;= \\frac{(4n-5)^2}{a_{2n-2}^2}a_{2n-1}^2 \\\\ \t    &amp;= M^2\\left(\\frac{(4n-5)(4n-1)}{4n-2}\\right)^2 \\\\ \t    &amp;= M^2\\left(\\frac{16n^2 -24n + 5}{4n-2}\\right)^2 \\\\ \t    &amp;= M^2\\left(\\frac{16n^2 -24n + 5}{16n^2 -24n + 8}\\right)^2 \t\t    \\cdot \\left(\\frac{16n^2 -24n + 8}{4n-2}\\right)^2 \\\\ \t    &amp;= M^2\\left(\\frac{16n^2 -24n + 5}{16n^2 -24n + 8}\\right)^2 \t\t\\cdot (4n-4)^2. \\end{align*}\" width=\"464\" height=\"256\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>which satisfies the required inequality provided:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/0/510f3a90661dde3a1951e05003f4d66f4b4c0b97.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}     M^2 &amp;\\le \\frac{16n^2 -24n + 8}{16n^2 -24n + 5} \\\\         &amp;= \\frac{(4n-4)(4n-2)}{(4n-5)(4n-1)} = M_{\\mathrm{max}}^2. \\end{align*}\" width=\"256\" height=\"90\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>The ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/a/a1a052ab440ff081fa0ac16208b7e31085c85c5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_{\\mathrm{max}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" />, between the largest and smallest\npossible value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/0/0f05a6d47399be7fbe2d86cf77838b8bd44e6c93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{2n-3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"40\" height=\"10\" /> is in fact the ratio between the largest and\nsmallest values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/b/1ab39d761413804680d26d972381f028001562f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"19\" height=\"13\" /> that yield a sequence that meets the\nconditions for at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/3/f93f5c51ea4b04b4992b003b39479f29018f6bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> terms.\n</p><p>In the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/b/6cb7ae00764b56ee2adf59e78a1ffde9685b80db.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> case, the equation for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/0/0f05a6d47399be7fbe2d86cf77838b8bd44e6c93.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{2n-3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"40\" height=\"10\" /> gives: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/4/c848b8cc6a9de986708ed23ad2fe8868ff2cc852.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1^2 = \\frac{21}{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"62\" height=\"37\" />. We will next consider what happens to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/e/48e0550a7330e0d906d836b56f0b7a0979b2ff39.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"15\" height=\"19\" />, and\nthe sequence of squares in general, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> increases.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/e/5fe09191496ae71095438808cdaa7fe01c9703c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{n,2i-1}, A_{n,2i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"99\" height=\"17\" /> denote the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/5/cd51b4c92d4e9876b12b11eb2c15a7543fc849b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i^{\\mathrm{th}}$\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> odd and\neven terms, respectively, of the unique sequence which satisfies our\noriginal equations and has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/3/f93f5c51ea4b04b4992b003b39479f29018f6bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> terms in total.\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/0/2f00c12ed478a1b09ef0a2de598424458616f20b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{n+1,2i-1}, A_{n+1,2i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"133\" height=\"17\" /> be the odd and even terms\nof the solution with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/3/44388ca8b97e320119ba1c111767d07b5617cfeb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" /> terms. We already noted that there\nmust exist a constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/1/eb12e06e542e73a4fa3b7237d8be00b2bef219c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" /> (that depends on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, but not on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />),\nsuch that:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/e/e5e5956e7f034d87bbcad4da8c5030e05eb9d95d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\begin{cases} \tA_{n+1,2i-1} = M_n\\cdot A_{n,2i-1},&amp; 1 \\le i \\le n \\\\ \tA_{n+1,2i} = \\dfrac{A_{n,2i}}{M_n},&amp; 1 \\le i \\le n \\end{cases}\\]\" width=\"299\" height=\"64\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>This constant is found explicitly by comparing the squares of the last\nterm <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/d/8fd0b9bbc67d58ccdf6ed6ae102669beb8ebc3d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{n,2n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"40\" height=\"17\" /> of the solution of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/3/f93f5c51ea4b04b4992b003b39479f29018f6bda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> with the square of\nthe third last term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/9/949eb2fe40273b9602a46bf21418241636c4aad7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{n+1,2n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"56\" height=\"17\" /> of the solution of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/3/44388ca8b97e320119ba1c111767d07b5617cfeb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2n+2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" />:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/f/8af87a818264960a4d72bdf3fb4d53115fb31194.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} \t(1/M_n)^2 &amp;= \\frac{A_{n+1,2n}^2}{A_{n,2n}^2} \\\\ \t      &amp;= \\frac{(4n+1)(4n+2)}{4n+3} \\cdot \\frac{4n-3}{(4n-2)(4n-1)} \\\\ \t      &amp;= \\frac{32n^3 - 14n - 3}{32n^3 - 14n + 3} \\end{align*}\" width=\"369\" height=\"143\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/d/b3d829cd4b86315a9d79bb9358b1cd3b1b1baab7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M_n &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"59\" height=\"15\" /> for all positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, and so for fixed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34857b3ba74ce5cd8607f3ebd23e9015908ada71.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"8\" height=\"14\" />, the\nodd index terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/2/252c3d754ee995e8d7a73b798c660e753ef505f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{n,2i-1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"17\" /> strictly increase with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, while\nthe even index terms <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/8/5d8c85e6f55c3d5aeee2090ef97347577fe04873.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{n,2i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"17\" /> decrease with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/7/317096889b84df0b72bf69ceb4c82208305f1b0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"46\" height=\"16\" />,\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/4/83414f946039300a4c773e96dcaf0dcb51e5f0a8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A_{n,1}^2 = \\frac{21}{10} \\prod_{k=2}^{n-1} M_k^2 =     \\frac{21}{10} \\cdot       \\prod_{k=2}^{n-1} \t\\frac{(k+\\frac{3}{4})(k-\\frac{1}{2})(k-\\frac{1}{4})} \t     {(k-\\frac{3}{4})(k+\\frac{1}{2})(k+\\frac{1}{4})}\\]\" width=\"399\" height=\"50\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>The product converges to a finite value even if taken infinitely\nfar, and we can conclude (by a simple continuity argument) that\nthere is a unique infinite positive sequence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/8/26827370e636313ad0eee459878239a6c6dfea01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" />, in which\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/8/2284455f0015e9d38faeba87664b8baba6519049.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{\\omega,i}A_{\\omega,i+1} = 2i+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"150\" height=\"17\" />, that satisfies all the\ninequalities <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/c/78ce21c9d84acbb3f07e9fb33b2f6d0cb8247fd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_{\\omega,i}A_{\\omega,j} &lt; i+j,\\; i \\le j - 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"208\" height=\"17\" />.  The\nsquare of the first term of the infinite sequence is:\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/1/9d1445dfead86f951fb40184848aad757f68fca2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} A_{\\omega,1}^2   &amp;=     \\frac{21}{10} \\cdot \\lim_{n \\to \\infty}       \\prod_{k=2}^{n-1} \t\\frac{(k+\\frac{3}{4})(k-\\frac{1}{2})(k-\\frac{1}{4})} \t     {(k-\\frac{3}{4})(k+\\frac{1}{2})(k+\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\   &amp;=     \\frac{21}{10} \\cdot \\lim_{n \\to \\infty}       \\left(       \\frac{\\Gamma(\\frac{5}{4})  \\Gamma(\\frac{5}{2})  \\Gamma(\\frac{9}{4})} \t   {\\Gamma(\\frac{11}{4}) \\Gamma(\\frac{3}{2})  \\Gamma(\\frac{7}{4})}\\cdot       \\frac{\\Gamma(n+\\frac{3}{4})\\Gamma(n-\\frac{1}{2})\\Gamma(n-\\frac{1}{4})} \t   {\\Gamma(n-\\frac{3}{4})\\Gamma(n+\\frac{1}{2})\\Gamma(n+\\frac{1}{4})}       \\right) \\\\   &amp;=     \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{\\Gamma^2(\\frac{1}{4})}{\\Gamma^2(\\frac{3}{4})} \\cdot       \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \t\\frac{\\Gamma(n+\\frac{3}{4})\\Gamma(n-\\frac{1}{2})\\Gamma(n-\\frac{1}{4})} \t     {\\Gamma(n-\\frac{3}{4})\\Gamma(n+\\frac{1}{2})\\Gamma(n+\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\   &amp;= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{\\Gamma^2(\\frac{1}{4})}{\\Gamma^2(\\frac{3}{4})}      \\cdot 1 \\quad \\mbox{(Stirling&#39;s approximation)}\\\\   &amp;= \\frac{\\Gamma^4(\\frac{1}{4})}{8\\pi^2}     = \\frac{\\pi}{\\mathrm{AGM}^2(\\sqrt{2}, 1)}     \\approx 2.1884396152264766\\ldots \\end{align*}\" width=\"497\" height=\"266\" />\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>In summary, if we set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/a/e9ad5921eb9ec606f7e404dfb2f2bbed55456477.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1 = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{\\mathrm{AGM}(\\sqrt{2}, 1)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -19px\" width=\"143\" height=\"45\" />,\nand then recursively set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/2/85274e1fbd279f3c13610d5da206963449a3c8b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_{i+1} = (2i + 1)/a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"140\" height=\"18\" />, we get an infinite\nsequence that, for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/7/897470a282b6b85fa5221eacd41369b9a157f491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" />, yields the maximum possible product\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/a/b7abb0326ed34191af4d64d1d33dc1f8b49a3d22.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1a_2\\cdots a_{2n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"85\" height=\"10\" />, subject to the conditions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/f/56fe154e9f5b8df8607c1a6adddfa7c009b16b72.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_ia_j \\le i+j,\\; 1 \\le i &lt; j \\le 2n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"221\" height=\"17\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO\" title=\"2010 USAMO\">2010 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=2010\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2010 USAMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\"